<p>Here on Open Culture, we’ve often featured the charming and informative posters of <em><a href="https://www.youtube.com/@DomainofScience">Domain of Science</a></em>. Each one maps out a different field of knowledge: there’s <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2017/12/the-map-of-biology-animation-shows-how-all-the-different-fields-in-biology-fit-together.html">biology</a>, <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2026/06/the-map-of-physics-animation.html">physics</a>, <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2017/05/the-map-of-chemistry-new-animation-summarizes-the-entire-field-of-chemistry-in-12-minutes.html">chemistry</a>, <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2020/08/the-map-of-quantum-physics-a-colorful-animation-explains-the-often-misunderstood-branch-of-science.html">quantum physics</a>, <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2022/05/the-animated-map-of-quantum-computing-a-visual-introduction-to-the-future-of-computing.html">quantum computing</a>, <a href="https://www.openculture.com/?p=1124762">fungi</a>, and many others besides. But with his latest map, <em>Domain of Science</em> creator Dominic Walliman has turned away from the scientific realm — or rather, he’s ventured into the larger intellectual territory out of which it emerged. His new <a href="https://domainofscience.com/the-map-of-philosophy">Map of Philosophy</a>, which he explains in <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=I0rkpF49Deo">the video above</a>, includes schools of thought going all the way back to 600 BC, and thinkers from antiquity like Thales, Laozi, Confucius, and the Buddha, who lived and died before science as we know it.</p>
<p>That hardly means that they’ve become irrelevant today. There still exist societies described as Confucian or Buddhist, of course, but in the Western world, we’re more likely to refer back to — and, in many cases, have read a bit of — the work of Socrates, Plato, and Aristotle. “They basically laid the intellectual foundation of Western philosophy, science, and political thought,” says Walliman. (He also reminds us that it was Socrates who said that the unexamined life is not worth living, which “hits pretty hard in the age of the distraction economy.”)</p>
